    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "584ccb6b_1cf7b2ba",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-17T20:54:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How is it unsafe here but ok at line 4340? Since the fd couldn\u0027t be patched, the buffer is corrupt (stale fds from sender) so we don\u0027t want the local proc to process it. What do you suggest?",
      "parentUuid": "dae9b0df_89ac6d76",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "38f02eab_77a0e081",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-17T21:04:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "4340 trigger a return if user-space passed in a bad pointer to receive data into. It is not a recoverable error. The error path you are adding could be hit by running out of file-descriptors. We should not corrupt the state of the receiving process in this case but fail the transaction that does not fit. You can write a test for this and pass more files in a single transaction than the limit for the receiving process (or you can see if there is a hwbinder test for this).",
      "parentUuid": "584ccb6b_1cf7b2ba",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "feae0ef1_fce3ceaf",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-17T22:14:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah ok, I\u0027m being to harsh on the local proc.\n\nWhat do you think is the appropriate way to handle this if it was an async transaction? The sender thinks everything was fine, but the receiver doesn\u0027t know anything about it and should probably not see it at all. Do senders assume guaranteed delivery of async transactions if they don\u0027t get an error and the target doesn\u0027t die?\n\n  if (reply) {\n    cmd\u003dBR_FAILED_REPLY\n  } else if (!async) {\n    // receiving a new transaction, local proc doesn\u0027t know anything\n    // about it, so reply with error and return noop command? Or can\n    // we return nothing?\n    send_failed_reply(...);\n    cmd\u003dBR_NOOP\n  } else {\n    ???\n    ignore it and send cmd\u003dBR_NOOP or nothing at all? Is it ok that the sender thinks this went\n    through? \n  }",
      "parentUuid": "38f02eab_77a0e081",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3e0b2e98_d46bfec7",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000205
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-18T00:00:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The driver currently guarantees delivery, so it is not unreasonable to assume there are senders that assume it. I don\u0027t know how common it is to pass fds in async transactions though as that is a good way to run out of file descriptors in the target process. Your change would make it less likely to fail, but undetectable. You should probably ask the framework team if they want a way to report this new failure case though. (also yes, you can return nothing here by not writing to the buffer)",
      "parentUuid": "feae0ef1_fce3ceaf",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a47d016_8228f6ba",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1013030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-20T09:27:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am inclined to think it\u0027s ok if they don\u0027t find out - if the target runs out of file descriptors only after the transaction lands in userspace, the Parcel code there would also reject the transaction without either the client or the server finding out, so it\u0027s not all that different from it failing here in the kernel.\n\nFor hwbinder it is a bit different because the receiver is responsible for cloning the fd and could detect the failure, but that doesn\u0027t change anything for the client.\n\nI checked the HIDL interfaces and AFAICT there are no users of oneway with file descriptors. In AIDL it\u0027s hard to tell because of Parcelables, which may contain file descriptors and are only described in code.",
      "parentUuid": "3e0b2e98_d46bfec7",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f3c73d1_b4cc8367",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-20T15:27:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are some cases of async transactions with fds (approx 0.7% of transactions with fds were async in my sample). Dianne indicated that fds in async transactions \"is used a lot\".\n\nMartijn/Arve, which do you like best of these options:\n1) write a kernel log message and otherwise ignore it\n2) deliver the transaction without the failed fds (fd \u003d\u003d -1) so they are surfaced in the target\n3) return an error on the ioctl which probably kills the target\n4) other idea?\n\nIt is sounding like 1 or 2 is probably best. Dianne didn\u0027t have an opinion.",
      "parentUuid": "9a47d016_8228f6ba",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1ab73a9c_a08fdc36",
        "filename": "drivers/android/binder.c",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 4328,
      "author": {
        "id": 1013030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-20T19:21:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d be in favor of #1; I don\u0027t think userspace will handle #2 too well, since (at least for framework binder) it will try to dup the fd and then fail. So we might as well not let it get that far. hwbinder userspace was written with the assumption the fd was valid also, so that\u0027s another reason to go for 1) IMHO. If we ever find a need to know about this failure on the calling side, I guess we can always add it then.",
      "parentUuid": "6f3c73d1_b4cc8367",
      "revId": "38c869776aa1b2b45b173edb8c333b2b16799108",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
